1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:03,000
They are grown by the millions,

2
00:00:03,001 --> 00:00:06,000
but the wrong ones will kill you.

3
00:00:06,001 --> 00:00:08,000
They bring about all kinds of goodness

4
00:00:08,001 --> 00:00:10,000
and all kinds of badness.

5
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:13,000
They're nature's great decomposers.

6
00:00:13,001 --> 00:00:19,000
They threaten to destroy your home and your body.

7
00:00:19,001 --> 00:00:22,000
They may even blow your mind.

8
00:00:22,001 --> 00:00:26,000
They've saved millions of lives and may save the world.

9
00:00:26,001 --> 00:00:29,000
They're the interface organisms between life and death.

10
00:00:29,001 --> 00:00:32,040
And in death, they'll consume us all.

11
00:00:32,140 --> 00:00:35,540
Now, mold and fungus on Modern Marvels.

12
00:00:46,660 --> 00:00:49,620
There is definitely a fungus among us

13
00:00:49,720 --> 00:00:52,320
every moment of the day.

14
00:00:52,420 --> 00:00:55,060
The fungal kingdom embraces an estimated million

15
00:00:55,160 --> 00:00:57,120
or more species.

16
00:00:57,121 --> 00:01:02,760
Within each of us, about 100 species alone thrive.

17
00:01:02,860 --> 00:01:06,500
Types of fungus most commonly known to us include yeast,

18
00:01:06,600 --> 00:01:10,240
mushrooms, and mold.

19
00:01:10,340 --> 00:01:15,100
When mold comes into our homes, it can be a killer.

20
00:01:15,200 --> 00:01:18,780
Over 25 million Americans suffer from some sort of allergic

21
00:01:18,880 --> 00:01:22,640
reaction to mold-related toxins called mycotoxins,

22
00:01:22,740 --> 00:01:25,820
released into the air.

23
00:01:25,821 --> 00:01:28,020
An undetermined number of these sufferers

24
00:01:28,120 --> 00:01:31,220
will die every year from overexposure.

25
00:01:31,320 --> 00:01:33,460
The fact is, any mold can be toxic

26
00:01:33,560 --> 00:01:35,160
if that person is allergic to it.

27
00:01:38,260 --> 00:01:39,600
If these guys look like they're

28
00:01:39,700 --> 00:01:41,940
suiting up to confront a biohazard,

29
00:01:42,040 --> 00:01:44,040
it's because they are.

30
00:01:44,140 --> 00:01:46,500
They're mold remediation specialists,

31
00:01:46,600 --> 00:01:47,880
and they have to protect themselves

32
00:01:47,980 --> 00:01:50,280
by preventing potentially harmful spores

33
00:01:50,380 --> 00:01:52,780
from invading minor cuts on their skin

34
00:01:52,880 --> 00:01:55,820
or entering their lungs.

35
00:01:55,821 --> 00:02:00,060
Mold removal or remediation is a $3 billion a year industry

36
00:02:00,160 --> 00:02:03,120
in the United States.

37
00:02:03,220 --> 00:02:06,560
The goal of remediation is to create a clean, dry area

38
00:02:06,660 --> 00:02:09,860
with a normal spore count.

39
00:02:09,960 --> 00:02:13,880
In other words, fewer spores inside the home than out.

40
00:02:13,980 --> 00:02:16,080
All we want to do is get it to a normal level.

41
00:02:16,180 --> 00:02:19,180
It is an impossible goal to get a mold-free structure

42
00:02:19,280 --> 00:02:21,480
in a residential home.

43
00:02:21,580 --> 00:02:23,920
To remove serious mold growth,

44
00:02:23,921 --> 00:02:28,920
the remediators often remove carpet and drywall from a room,

45
00:02:29,020 --> 00:02:31,700
sometimes treating the wood structure behind it.

46
00:02:31,800 --> 00:02:34,460
There are several ways you can treat the studs depending

47
00:02:34,560 --> 00:02:36,000
on the severity of the damage.

48
00:02:36,100 --> 00:02:37,400
You can sand them.

49
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:41,500
In some cases, you might even be able to wipe them down.

50
00:02:41,600 --> 00:02:46,780
For large jobs, they can also use dry ice blasting.

51
00:02:46,880 --> 00:02:48,940
The combination of the extreme cold

52
00:02:49,040 --> 00:02:52,620
and the force of the blast causes the mold to shrink

53
00:02:52,621 --> 00:02:54,860
shrink and detach itself from the surface.

54
00:02:58,060 --> 00:03:01,360
Once the remediators clean and treat the infested areas,

55
00:03:01,361 --> 00:03:02,620
the homeowner can rebuild.

56
00:03:05,340 --> 00:03:07,300
The work of the remediators is actually

57
00:03:07,301 --> 00:03:08,700
the last step in the process.

58
00:03:11,440 --> 00:03:14,780
Before the remediation, a mold specialist must come in

59
00:03:14,781 --> 00:03:16,980
and evaluate how serious the problem is.

60
00:03:19,520 --> 00:03:21,320
Once he confirms the presence of mold

61
00:03:21,321 --> 00:03:24,860
area of the home, the consultant collects surface and air samples

62
00:03:24,861 --> 00:03:30,860
from nearby rooms to locate any cross-contamination.

63
00:03:30,861 --> 00:03:33,760
These samples make their way to an independent lab for spore

64
00:03:33,761 --> 00:03:36,760
count analysis, as well as mold identification.

65
00:03:39,780 --> 00:03:43,480
While the spore is the mold's way of traveling through the air,

66
00:03:43,481 --> 00:03:46,080
it is also its main reproductive body,

67
00:03:46,081 --> 00:03:48,920
similar to a plant's seed.

68
00:03:48,921 --> 00:03:52,760
From the spore, a hypha, or branch, grows.

69
00:03:52,860 --> 00:03:55,760
A collective of these hyphae, known as mycelium,

70
00:03:55,860 --> 00:03:59,060
is the mold's root-like system, the part of the mold

71
00:03:59,160 --> 00:04:01,260
visible to the naked eye.

72
00:04:01,360 --> 00:04:04,840
The mycelium emit enzymes to break down nearby food,

73
00:04:04,940 --> 00:04:08,580
in this case, the wall itself, in order to absorb or ingest

74
00:04:08,680 --> 00:04:09,980
nutrients.

75
00:04:10,080 --> 00:04:13,180
More spores, more mold.

76
00:04:13,280 --> 00:04:16,220
They produce colonies, and they spread their spores out.

77
00:04:16,320 --> 00:04:18,920
Usually, that's in a circular pattern.

78
00:04:18,921 --> 00:04:22,260
And while many people fear the notorious black mold,

79
00:04:22,360 --> 00:04:24,760
actually many molds are black.

80
00:04:24,860 --> 00:04:27,420
And many other colorful molds can be just as dangerous.

81
00:04:30,260 --> 00:04:34,600
Like a snake when threatened, some molds release toxins

82
00:04:34,700 --> 00:04:37,180
as means of self-defense.

83
00:04:37,280 --> 00:04:40,640
If these mycotoxins are in high enough concentration,

84
00:04:40,740 --> 00:04:44,580
the effect on a nearby human can be devastating.

85
00:04:44,680 --> 00:04:47,680
The problem with mold is it affects everyone differently.

86
00:04:47,681 --> 00:04:50,580
So what may bother me may not necessarily bother you.

87
00:04:50,680 --> 00:04:52,980
Generally, mold affects the very young, the very old,

88
00:04:53,080 --> 00:04:56,420
and anyone that's immunocompromised.

89
00:04:56,520 --> 00:05:01,020
Mold needs two simple ingredients to thrive.

90
00:05:01,120 --> 00:05:05,140
Virtually any organic substance will do for the food.

91
00:05:05,240 --> 00:05:09,000
The only thing we can try to control is the water.

92
00:05:09,100 --> 00:05:13,500
But sometimes nature has a way of spoiling our best efforts.

93
00:05:13,600 --> 00:05:15,980
During a series of heavy rains, the water

94
00:05:15,981 --> 00:05:17,580
The water came up through the floor.

95
00:05:17,980 --> 00:05:20,460
It began to be absorbed into the building materials.

96
00:05:20,940 --> 00:05:23,880
As a result of the building materials being cellulose,

97
00:05:24,320 --> 00:05:27,100
mold began to eat that as a food source,

98
00:05:27,360 --> 00:05:28,220
and it began to grow.

99
00:05:29,320 --> 00:05:30,800
Another contributing factor

100
00:05:30,801 --> 00:05:33,960
to many basement mold problems, humidity.

101
00:05:34,400 --> 00:05:36,360
In this case, the relative humidity in the building

102
00:05:36,361 --> 00:05:40,240
is 84%, and the temperature is 82 degrees Fahrenheit.

103
00:05:40,900 --> 00:05:42,560
You want a relative humidity in a structure

104
00:05:42,561 --> 00:05:44,240
between 40 and 60%.

105
00:05:44,241 --> 00:05:46,880
The faster the area is dried, the less likely you are

106
00:05:46,980 --> 00:05:50,480
of having this type of a situation.

107
00:05:50,580 --> 00:05:53,880
While this wall will have to be removed by specialists,

108
00:05:53,980 --> 00:05:56,380
a more common form of mold in many homes

109
00:05:56,480 --> 00:06:01,120
is found in the bathroom, often referred to as mildew.

110
00:06:01,220 --> 00:06:04,200
It embodies all the risks of other mold invasions,

111
00:06:04,300 --> 00:06:06,000
but is usually more treatable.

112
00:06:06,100 --> 00:06:07,500
What's the cause of this?

113
00:06:07,600 --> 00:06:10,340
Almost always the cause is improper ventilation.

114
00:06:10,440 --> 00:06:13,740
And you do simple things to help prevent this from reoccurring,

115
00:06:13,741 --> 00:06:16,380
leaving the shower door or curtain open when you're finished

116
00:06:16,480 --> 00:06:19,420
is a great first step.

117
00:06:19,520 --> 00:06:21,080
When cleaning mold of any size,

118
00:06:21,180 --> 00:06:24,420
there is at least one rule of thumb.

119
00:06:24,520 --> 00:06:27,820
The one I would say don't use is bleach.

120
00:06:27,920 --> 00:06:29,320
The bleach stays on the very surface.

121
00:06:29,420 --> 00:06:31,160
So when you do use bleach to clean it,

122
00:06:31,260 --> 00:06:32,720
bleach is always mixed in water.

123
00:06:32,820 --> 00:06:35,200
The water is doing more damage than the bleach is actually

124
00:06:35,300 --> 00:06:36,660
doing beneficial.

125
00:06:36,760 --> 00:06:39,840
First step is fix the problem, dry things out,

126
00:06:39,940 --> 00:06:42,660
kill it so it doesn't come back, and then clean it with any

127
00:06:42,661 --> 00:06:45,160
you want to use it with, except bleach.

128
00:06:48,160 --> 00:06:50,660
Outside the confines of our homes,

129
00:06:50,760 --> 00:06:54,400
fungi find plenty of places to thrive in nature.

130
00:06:54,500 --> 00:06:57,700
85% of all plants, especially trees,

131
00:06:57,800 --> 00:07:00,820
have developed a relationship that benefits both sides,

132
00:07:00,920 --> 00:07:04,320
referred to as symbiotic.

133
00:07:04,420 --> 00:07:07,920
The fungi's mycelium acts as nature's great decomposer.

134
00:07:07,921 --> 00:07:10,920
It breaks down leaves and other organisms

135
00:07:11,020 --> 00:07:14,920
into a rich compost, which nourishes the tree.

136
00:07:15,020 --> 00:07:17,660
It's a hidden landscape to most people,

137
00:07:17,760 --> 00:07:20,660
but here is mycelium surfacing,

138
00:07:20,760 --> 00:07:25,280
and it is all just beneath the top layers.

139
00:07:25,380 --> 00:07:30,480
There can be more than 300 miles of mycelium

140
00:07:30,580 --> 00:07:32,620
underneath my hand.

141
00:07:32,720 --> 00:07:35,980
There can be more than eight miles in a car.

142
00:07:35,981 --> 00:07:39,820
They can be more than eight miles and a cubic inch.

143
00:07:39,821 --> 00:07:45,480
And this mycelium is because the tree fall,

144
00:07:45,481 --> 00:07:47,960
the needles feeding the mycelial mat,

145
00:07:47,961 --> 00:07:51,060
and beneath the mycelium, you have dirt.

146
00:07:51,061 --> 00:07:53,860
So these are the soil magicians of nature.

147
00:07:53,861 --> 00:07:55,500
They create soils.

148
00:07:55,501 --> 00:07:59,800
They're the interface organisms between life and death.

149
00:07:59,801 --> 00:08:02,680
How effective is this symbiotic relationship?

150
00:08:02,681 --> 00:08:04,380
Consider this.

151
00:08:04,381 --> 00:08:05,720
The largest organism in the world

152
00:08:05,721 --> 00:08:07,540
is the mycelial mass in eastern Oregon,

153
00:08:07,541 --> 00:08:11,720
over 2,200 acres in size, more than 2,000 years old,

154
00:08:11,721 --> 00:08:15,020
and that's one cell wall thick.

155
00:08:15,021 --> 00:08:19,120
Scientists consider this 35,000 ton mycelium giant,

156
00:08:19,121 --> 00:08:21,740
aptly called the humongous fungus,

157
00:08:21,741 --> 00:08:25,040
as a crucial part of the forest ecosystem.

158
00:08:25,041 --> 00:08:28,740
It creates food and space for plants and animals to grow

159
00:08:28,741 --> 00:08:32,880
by killing and breaking down ancient, weakened trees.

160
00:08:32,881 --> 00:08:36,480
Meanwhile, other destructive fungi invade and destroy food

161
00:08:36,580 --> 00:08:37,880
crops.

162
00:08:37,980 --> 00:08:43,820
The cost to US farmers can reach over $20 billion a year.

163
00:08:43,920 --> 00:08:48,220
Smut fungi cause cereal crops such as corn to swell and fill

164
00:08:48,320 --> 00:08:50,540
with a sooty mold.

165
00:08:50,640 --> 00:08:53,040
Powdery mildew is one of the worst grape diseases

166
00:08:53,140 --> 00:08:55,140
worldwide.

167
00:08:55,240 --> 00:08:58,340
And then there is fusarium head blight of wheat and barley

168
00:08:58,440 --> 00:08:59,640
crops.

169
00:08:59,740 --> 00:09:02,080
In the 1990s alone in the United States,

170
00:09:02,081 --> 00:09:06,080
caused by Fusarium graminiarum was estimated to be

171
00:09:06,180 --> 00:09:09,720
at approximately $1.3 billion of direct damage

172
00:09:09,820 --> 00:09:13,060
to the farmers and indirect damage into the $4 billion

173
00:09:13,160 --> 00:09:15,060
range.

174
00:09:15,160 --> 00:09:18,800
While some fungi are busy destroying crops,

175
00:09:18,900 --> 00:09:23,200
other fungi are busy destroying them.

176
00:09:23,300 --> 00:09:26,700
Researchers at the USDA's National Center for Agricultural

177
00:09:26,800 --> 00:09:30,340
Utilization Research are finding ways to take advantage

178
00:09:30,341 --> 00:09:33,340
advantage of the natural battle of fungus against fungus.

179
00:09:37,340 --> 00:09:40,980
They use fungi to create new environmentally safe fungicides

180
00:09:41,080 --> 00:09:46,420
and insecticides, referred to as biocontrols.

181
00:09:46,520 --> 00:09:48,920
We're looking to take some of these natural interactions,

182
00:09:49,020 --> 00:09:52,360
find out how they work, mass produce those organisms,

183
00:09:52,460 --> 00:09:54,300
and then take them back out into the field

184
00:09:54,400 --> 00:09:57,140
so they can have that effect over a larger acreage.

185
00:10:00,340 --> 00:10:04,280
They're also looking to fungi to attack insect pests.

186
00:10:04,281 --> 00:10:08,040
There are many fungi that infect and kill insects.

187
00:10:08,041 --> 00:10:11,600
They use insects as a source of food.

188
00:10:11,601 --> 00:10:14,140
And that's what we're trying to exploit

189
00:10:14,141 --> 00:10:18,340
in terms of biological control or control of insects

190
00:10:18,341 --> 00:10:21,240
with fungi.

191
00:10:21,241 --> 00:10:23,300
One insect that Mark Jackson is studying,

192
00:10:23,301 --> 00:10:26,240
along with USDA scientists in New Orleans,

193
00:10:26,241 --> 00:10:29,440
is the subterranean termite.

194
00:10:29,441 --> 00:10:35,720
Subterranean termites are a big problem, not in agriculture, but in the urban setting,

195
00:10:35,721 --> 00:10:42,760
and they cause upward of $2 billion a year in damage and in money spent to control these

196
00:10:42,761 --> 00:10:44,680
insect pests.

197
00:10:44,681 --> 00:10:50,720
Fungi that infect insects are very unique in that they're able to actually penetrate

198
00:10:50,721 --> 00:10:57,720
through the insect cuticle, consume the insect, and then produce spores on the surface of

199
00:10:57,721 --> 00:10:58,720
the insect.

200
00:10:59,440 --> 00:11:02,480
After several days, the termite finally disappears

201
00:11:02,580 --> 00:11:05,180
in a mass of fungus.

202
00:11:05,280 --> 00:11:07,880
It's hoped that this will infect other termites

203
00:11:07,980 --> 00:11:13,680
in the nest and lead to collapse of the termite nest.

204
00:11:13,780 --> 00:11:17,100
Different fungi infect insects in ways that are as chilling

205
00:11:17,200 --> 00:11:19,500
as a monster movie.

206
00:11:19,600 --> 00:11:22,700
In the case of cordyceps fungi,

207
00:11:22,800 --> 00:11:26,500
they actually produce a fruiting body, which is a mushroom that

208
00:11:26,600 --> 00:11:29,440
comes off the insect.

209
00:11:29,441 --> 00:11:33,240
This mushroom not too appetizing?

210
00:11:33,340 --> 00:11:37,640
Maybe some moldy beans might be more your taste.

211
00:11:37,740 --> 00:11:40,180
In some cultures, it's considered a staple.

212
00:11:44,580 --> 00:11:48,000
Fungi are everywhere, in the air around us,

213
00:11:48,100 --> 00:11:51,200
the soil beneath our feet, and sometimes right

214
00:11:51,300 --> 00:11:52,200
in our refrigerators.

215
00:11:54,800 --> 00:11:58,540
And some of us subscribe to the, if you can't beat it,

216
00:11:58,541 --> 00:12:02,280
Edith School of Mold and Fungus.

217
00:12:02,380 --> 00:12:07,040
Take the Haines Celestial West Soy Plant in Boulder, Colorado.

218
00:12:07,140 --> 00:12:11,920
It produces over 150 tons of tempeh yearly.

219
00:12:12,020 --> 00:12:14,720
A combination of soybeans, water,

220
00:12:14,820 --> 00:12:19,020
and a beneficial fungus called Rhizopus oligosporus,

221
00:12:19,120 --> 00:12:24,300
tempeh is a rich source of both fiber and protein.

222
00:12:24,400 --> 00:12:26,360
A food staple of Indonesian cooking

223
00:12:26,361 --> 00:12:28,860
for over 1,000 years.

224
00:12:28,960 --> 00:12:30,860
This fungal-based food spread its way

225
00:12:30,960 --> 00:12:35,000
into the American diet in the 1950s.

226
00:12:35,100 --> 00:12:38,000
We start with a cracked, de-hulled soybean

227
00:12:38,100 --> 00:12:40,100
that are shipped to us this way.

228
00:12:40,200 --> 00:12:44,720
The seed coat has been removed because the rhizopus oligosporis

229
00:12:44,820 --> 00:12:47,920
won't bind fully to the seed coat.

230
00:12:48,020 --> 00:12:50,260
Haynes Celestial combines cooked soybeans

231
00:12:50,360 --> 00:12:51,860
with the mold in a mixer.

232
00:12:51,861 --> 00:12:55,160
mixer.

233
00:12:55,161 --> 00:12:59,260
Then workers bag and tamp or press this new product

234
00:12:59,261 --> 00:13:01,400
into smooth, flat cakes.

235
00:13:01,401 --> 00:13:04,000
The importance of tamping the tempeh is you want

236
00:13:04,001 --> 00:13:06,340
to have a flat cake.

237
00:13:06,341 --> 00:13:09,080
And because of the closeness, the mold can grow

238
00:13:09,081 --> 00:13:11,980
around the beans better.

239
00:13:11,981 --> 00:13:15,160
Over the next 18 to 24 hours, mold

240
00:13:15,161 --> 00:13:18,620
grows on the beans inside this temperature-controlled room,

241
00:13:18,621 --> 00:13:20,360
the incubator.

242
00:13:20,361 --> 00:13:28,280
Tempeh is from Indonesia, and their average temperature is about 77 degrees all the time.

243
00:13:28,980 --> 00:13:33,060
The incubator helps replicate that climate in a more controlled setting.

244
00:13:34,460 --> 00:13:40,900
Tempeh is a living, breathing organism, and just like us, it requires oxygen.

245
00:13:41,520 --> 00:13:45,580
Also, we generate heat, and if you were to feel this piece of tempeh,

246
00:13:45,900 --> 00:13:49,180
you would notice that it's generating quite a bit of heat.

247
00:13:50,360 --> 00:13:53,000
It takes someone who really knows tempeh to judge when

248
00:13:53,100 --> 00:13:56,400
the mold growth has reached its peak.

249
00:13:56,500 --> 00:14:00,300
If I were to break this open and you look on the inside,

250
00:14:00,400 --> 00:14:05,500
you can see that the mycelium, or the cottony part of the tempeh,

251
00:14:05,600 --> 00:14:09,320
has firmly bound the beans together.

252
00:14:09,420 --> 00:14:11,320
In order to inhibit further growth,

253
00:14:11,420 --> 00:14:14,820
the tempeh must enter a steam cabinet.

254
00:14:14,920 --> 00:14:19,660
That kills off the fungus and stops its further development.

255
00:14:19,661 --> 00:14:22,960
At that point, we bring it from the steam cabinet

256
00:14:23,060 --> 00:14:28,800
into the cooler, where we chill it down for final packaging.

257
00:14:28,900 --> 00:14:31,440
After packaging, the facility heat treats the tempeh

258
00:14:31,540 --> 00:14:36,880
again, increasing its shelf life to over 70 days.

259
00:14:36,980 --> 00:14:41,340
Then it's chilled and sent off to the supermarket.

260
00:14:41,440 --> 00:14:43,820
With a bit of a nutty taste and texture,

261
00:14:43,920 --> 00:14:47,280
tempeh is a master of disguise, capable of morphing

262
00:14:47,281 --> 00:14:50,380
into many popular dishes.

263
00:14:50,480 --> 00:14:52,980
Across the Atlantic from West Soy,

264
00:14:53,080 --> 00:14:55,520
a company in the United Kingdom figured out

265
00:14:55,620 --> 00:14:57,900
how to skip the beans and turn mold

266
00:14:58,000 --> 00:15:02,300
into one of the most popular meatless products in Europe,

267
00:15:02,400 --> 00:15:04,160
corn.

268
00:15:04,260 --> 00:15:08,000
The United Kingdom alone consumes over 500,000 corn-based meals

269
00:15:08,100 --> 00:15:10,840
every day.

270
00:15:10,940 --> 00:15:14,780
Referred to as a mycoprotein, its fibrous fungal structure

271
00:15:14,781 --> 00:15:19,580
helps give corn products a meat-like texture.

272
00:15:19,680 --> 00:15:22,120
And with the right seasonings, well,

273
00:15:22,220 --> 00:15:24,020
they swear it tastes just like chicken.

274
00:15:26,920 --> 00:15:30,320
Not familiar with these mold-inspired foods?

275
00:15:30,420 --> 00:15:34,240
Perhaps you've taken a bite of mold like this.

276
00:15:34,340 --> 00:15:36,840
Commonly used to ripen many cheeses,

277
00:15:36,940 --> 00:15:40,880
it may appear as the white rind on brie or camembert,

278
00:15:40,980 --> 00:15:44,780
or the blue highlights within blue cheese or gorgonzola.

279
00:15:44,781 --> 00:15:47,820
On Roquefort, you can actually see where the cheese maker

280
00:15:47,920 --> 00:15:50,420
injected it right in.

281
00:15:50,520 --> 00:15:52,820
While mold might be sounding tasty,

282
00:15:52,920 --> 00:15:56,520
the most familiar edible fungi, the mushroom,

283
00:15:56,620 --> 00:15:59,720
still reigns supreme.

284
00:15:59,820 --> 00:16:03,840
In the United States, 65% of all cultivated mushrooms

285
00:16:03,940 --> 00:16:05,940
come from Pennsylvania.

286
00:16:06,040 --> 00:16:10,040
Some come from Creekside Farms outside of Worthington.

287
00:16:10,140 --> 00:16:12,440
The largest mushroom farm in the world,

288
00:16:12,441 --> 00:16:17,440
Creekside primarily harvests button mushrooms within 150

289
00:16:17,540 --> 00:16:18,980
miles of limestone tunnels.

290
00:16:21,320 --> 00:16:25,380
This adds up to nearly 800 acres of growing space, 300 feet

291
00:16:25,480 --> 00:16:27,680
below the Earth's surface.

292
00:16:27,780 --> 00:16:29,560
The neat thing about growing mushrooms

293
00:16:29,660 --> 00:16:33,120
at this particular facility is that the temperature is pretty

294
00:16:33,220 --> 00:16:35,800
much consistent all year round.

295
00:16:35,900 --> 00:16:38,600
And we maintain about a 62 degree temperature

296
00:16:38,601 --> 00:16:42,440
and about a 95% humidity, which allows mushrooms

297
00:16:42,540 --> 00:16:47,640
to just recycle themselves pretty much every 24 hours.

298
00:16:47,740 --> 00:16:50,340
Because all mushrooms mature at different rates,

299
00:16:50,440 --> 00:16:53,120
they must be hand-picked.

300
00:16:53,220 --> 00:16:57,880
Creekside harvests over 26 million pounds every year.

301
00:16:57,980 --> 00:17:02,920
But 280 miles away, you'll find Kennett Square, the self-proclaimed

302
00:17:03,020 --> 00:17:06,220
mushroom capital of the world.

303
00:17:06,221 --> 00:17:08,220
The state's highest concentration of farms

304
00:17:08,320 --> 00:17:12,560
is located here, including Phillips Mushroom Farms,

305
00:17:12,660 --> 00:17:14,660
the largest producer of specialty mushrooms.

306
00:17:18,060 --> 00:17:22,760
Here, extraordinary mushrooms burst out of bags, bottles,

307
00:17:22,860 --> 00:17:28,680
and beds with names like maitake, pom-pom, or lion's mane,

308
00:17:28,780 --> 00:17:35,180
shiitake, royal trumpet, oyster, inyoki, cremini,

309
00:17:35,181 --> 00:17:39,080
and their biggest seller, Portobello.

310
00:17:39,180 --> 00:17:41,920
We produce about 8.5 million pounds of mushrooms a year.

311
00:17:42,020 --> 00:17:43,920
That's all varieties of mushrooms.

312
00:17:44,020 --> 00:17:48,920
And it's a challenge, but it's fun.

313
00:17:49,020 --> 00:17:51,860
This has accomplished over roughly 1 million square feet

314
00:17:51,960 --> 00:17:57,840
of growing space, separated into 125 60-foot-long,

315
00:17:57,940 --> 00:18:02,680
windowless, temperature-controlled growing houses.

316
00:18:02,681 --> 00:18:05,580
In this room, we grow both cremini and portobello.

317
00:18:05,680 --> 00:18:07,680
Every portobello starts as a cremini.

318
00:18:07,780 --> 00:18:11,160
A portobello is just a mature mushroom that we allow to grow,

319
00:18:11,260 --> 00:18:14,960
and it gets to the point where the veil separates

320
00:18:15,060 --> 00:18:17,000
and exposes the gill.

321
00:18:17,100 --> 00:18:20,660
On a closed mushroom, we have the stem and the cap,

322
00:18:20,760 --> 00:18:23,300
and here tucked inside is the gill.

323
00:18:25,700 --> 00:18:28,880
But before a mushroom can grow up to be a portobello,

324
00:18:28,980 --> 00:18:31,380
it must first be spawned in a very sterile,

325
00:18:31,381 --> 00:18:32,380
very restricted area.

326
00:18:35,880 --> 00:18:38,380
Spawn is the process of uniting a particular fungus

327
00:18:38,480 --> 00:18:42,260
with a carrier, such as sawdust or millet grain,

328
00:18:42,360 --> 00:18:44,920
to create a master culture.

329
00:18:45,020 --> 00:18:50,060
It's then transferred to a bag of sterilized grain.

330
00:18:50,160 --> 00:18:52,760
This becomes the actual spawn that we would use

331
00:18:52,860 --> 00:18:55,640
to produce mushrooms.

332
00:18:55,740 --> 00:18:58,100
The spawn is then added to a substrate,

333
00:18:58,200 --> 00:19:01,380
or base of growth.

334
00:19:01,381 --> 00:19:08,380
In the case of Cromini and Portabellas, the substrates are large beds of compost.

335
00:19:08,381 --> 00:19:14,380
The farm then creates optimal growing conditions by carefully adjusting temperature, humidity,

336
00:19:14,381 --> 00:19:19,380
fresh air, and carbon dioxide levels in the room.

337
00:19:19,381 --> 00:19:23,675
This white fungus that you see here is actually the mushroom fungus.

338
00:19:23,676 --> 00:19:25,380
What we eat is the fruiting

339
00:19:25,381 --> 00:19:28,380
body of the fungus. That's the actual mushroom.

340
00:19:28,381 --> 00:19:33,380
It's up to harvesters to decide when each mushroom is ready for the market.

341
00:19:33,381 --> 00:19:37,380
A mushroom actually can double in size in 24 hours.

342
00:19:37,381 --> 00:19:39,380
And the bigger it is, the more it weighs.

343
00:19:39,381 --> 00:19:43,380
Since we get paid by the pound, the more pounds you pick, the better off you're going to be.

344
00:19:43,381 --> 00:19:49,380
One of the perks of the job is that I get to come in here and just grab a mushroom off the bed.

345
00:19:49,381 --> 00:19:54,380
Bite the top right off of it. It's great.

346
00:19:54,381 --> 00:19:56,340
One of the great ways to have a portobello

347
00:19:56,440 --> 00:20:00,020
is just to pop the stem out, marinate it

348
00:20:00,120 --> 00:20:03,120
in your favorite Italian dressing for about 15 minutes,

349
00:20:03,220 --> 00:20:06,120
throw it on the grill, a little Asiago cheese,

350
00:20:06,220 --> 00:20:11,740
a little roasted red pepper, put in a nice crunchy baguette.

351
00:20:11,840 --> 00:20:12,940
Bellissimo.

352
00:20:13,040 --> 00:20:16,540
Can't eat any better than that.

353
00:20:16,640 --> 00:20:20,040
To the wild mushroom enthusiast and connoisseur,

354
00:20:20,140 --> 00:20:22,940
nothing compares to the hunt.

355
00:20:22,941 --> 00:20:25,580
And for those who can properly identify which mushrooms

356
00:20:25,680 --> 00:20:30,040
are safe and delicious, hunting can be a profitable hobby.

357
00:20:30,140 --> 00:20:34,480
Hard-to-find varieties fetch as much as $650 a pound.

358
00:20:34,580 --> 00:20:36,480
It's really becoming very popular.

359
00:20:36,580 --> 00:20:41,100
And it's very fun because it's like adult Easter egg hunting.

360
00:20:41,200 --> 00:20:43,100
The edible trophies for many fungi

361
00:20:43,200 --> 00:20:47,500
hunters include chanterelle, pink-tipped coral,

362
00:20:47,600 --> 00:20:52,940
fried chicken mushrooms, King bolete, also known as porcini,

363
00:20:52,941 --> 00:20:56,940
And morel, one of the most picked of the wild mushrooms.

364
00:21:00,280 --> 00:21:02,280
Morel hunters often seek these fungi

365
00:21:02,380 --> 00:21:05,580
in burned areas of evergreen forests.

366
00:21:05,680 --> 00:21:07,280
The very following spring, there'll

367
00:21:07,380 --> 00:21:08,780
be a flush of morel.

368
00:21:08,880 --> 00:21:12,300
You might have 4,000 to 5,000 pickers in one burn site area

369
00:21:12,400 --> 00:21:14,300
if it's a big enough burn.

370
00:21:14,400 --> 00:21:17,040
This bag here probably weighs about five pounds,

371
00:21:17,140 --> 00:21:21,100
and this is one of three bags we got in about an hour today.

372
00:21:21,101 --> 00:21:24,500
Fresh wild morels might bring the hunter as much as $120

373
00:21:24,600 --> 00:21:25,400
per pound.

374
00:21:28,200 --> 00:21:30,440
But nothing compares to the most prized fungi

375
00:21:30,540 --> 00:21:34,340
of them all, the truffle.

376
00:21:34,440 --> 00:21:37,680
In fact, a 3.3 pound Italian white truffle

377
00:21:37,780 --> 00:21:41,760
brought over $300,000 at auction.

378
00:21:41,860 --> 00:21:45,860
If we had it here, people could smell it 100 feet away.

379
00:21:45,960 --> 00:21:48,760
Due to their high cost and strong flavor,

380
00:21:48,761 --> 00:21:51,760
They're often served in thin slices within other dishes.

381
00:21:53,760 --> 00:21:56,760
Money is of little consequence when it comes to dangerous

382
00:21:56,761 --> 00:21:59,400
and magical mushrooms.

383
00:21:59,401 --> 00:22:02,400
But could they have really caused Santa's reindeer to fly?

384
00:22:07,520 --> 00:22:09,620
Thousands of species of wild mushrooms

385
00:22:09,621 --> 00:22:12,120
grow in North America.

386
00:22:12,121 --> 00:22:17,620
Of these, researchers consider around 100 as being toxic,

387
00:22:17,621 --> 00:22:20,820
Meaning they can affect humans mentally, physically,

388
00:22:20,920 --> 00:22:23,920
and even fatally.

389
00:22:24,020 --> 00:22:25,460
Picking and eating the wrong mushroom

390
00:22:25,560 --> 00:22:28,200
can mean the difference between a tasty meal

391
00:22:28,300 --> 00:22:31,460
and extreme pain or even death.

392
00:22:31,560 --> 00:22:33,700
It's critically important that people

393
00:22:33,800 --> 00:22:35,400
know which mushrooms are poisonous

394
00:22:35,500 --> 00:22:38,100
versus which ones are edible, obviously.

395
00:22:38,200 --> 00:22:41,840
If you eat a mushroom and you get sick within two hours,

396
00:22:41,940 --> 00:22:44,420
probably not a very dangerous mushroom.

397
00:22:44,520 --> 00:22:45,740
You'll have stomach cramps.

398
00:22:45,741 --> 00:22:48,240
you may have diarrhea, you may throw up.

399
00:22:48,340 --> 00:22:52,220
But that category of toxins that cause an immediate reaction

400
00:22:52,320 --> 00:22:54,180
typically are not life-threatening.

401
00:22:54,280 --> 00:22:57,020
If you have these symptoms 12 hours or later,

402
00:22:57,120 --> 00:23:00,580
then you may well have entered into a group of highly toxic,

403
00:23:00,680 --> 00:23:05,400
deadly poisonous mushrooms that we all hear about.

404
00:23:05,500 --> 00:23:07,300
Toxins from life-threatening mushrooms,

405
00:23:07,400 --> 00:23:10,100
such as the aminidophiloides, or death count,

406
00:23:10,200 --> 00:23:12,100
can damage the kidneys and liver.

407
00:23:15,740 --> 00:23:18,940
That happened in the 1950s with a similar mushroom

408
00:23:19,040 --> 00:23:23,640
in Poland, when over 100 people in a small community suddenly

409
00:23:23,740 --> 00:23:26,420
and mysteriously became ill.

410
00:23:26,520 --> 00:23:29,220
11 of them died.

411
00:23:29,320 --> 00:23:31,820
The mushroom that poisoned them produced no symptoms

412
00:23:31,920 --> 00:23:34,460
until nearly two weeks after ingestion.

413
00:23:34,560 --> 00:23:37,400
It's been suggested it was a perfect assassin's mushroom

414
00:23:37,500 --> 00:23:41,400
because you could serve it, you know, no symptoms at all.

415
00:23:41,500 --> 00:23:44,460
And then you go away, and two weeks later, your victim

416
00:23:44,461 --> 00:23:48,400
your victim, you know, falls dead.

417
00:23:48,401 --> 00:23:50,460
Perhaps Roman Empress Agrippina

418
00:23:50,461 --> 00:23:52,300
had the same idea when thinking of a way

419
00:23:52,301 --> 00:23:56,440
to get her son on the throne in 54 AD.

420
00:23:56,441 --> 00:23:59,340
Her husband, Emperor Claudius, reportedly died

421
00:23:59,341 --> 00:24:03,220
of mysterious causes after eating a plate of mushrooms.

422
00:24:03,221 --> 00:24:05,980
But then so did Alexander I of Russia

423
00:24:05,981 --> 00:24:09,280
and Holy Roman Emperor Charles VI.

424
00:24:09,281 --> 00:24:11,660
Never eat a mushroom unless it's properly identified.

425
00:24:11,661 --> 00:24:14,080
Always be with a mushroom expert.

426
00:24:14,081 --> 00:24:17,420
of your mushrooms so you don't eat all of them.

427
00:24:17,520 --> 00:24:19,820
Another mushroom rarely causes death,

428
00:24:19,920 --> 00:24:23,820
but is better known for its use as a psychoactive substance.

429
00:24:23,920 --> 00:24:26,400
Amnida muscaria, the famous soma mushroom,

430
00:24:26,500 --> 00:24:28,700
the red mushroom with the white dots that you see associated

431
00:24:28,800 --> 00:24:31,260
with Santa Claus and Christmas and fir trees.

432
00:24:31,360 --> 00:24:34,500
In Siberia, there's been a long history of the use of this

433
00:24:34,600 --> 00:24:36,140
mushroom by shamans.

434
00:24:36,240 --> 00:24:38,400
Amnida muscaria contains many compounds,

435
00:24:38,500 --> 00:24:41,740
but three notable ones are ebotanic acid, musumol,

436
00:24:41,840 --> 00:24:42,780
and muscarin.

437
00:24:42,781 --> 00:24:44,440
muscarin.

438
00:24:44,540 --> 00:24:47,020
Muscarin can cause excessive salivation,

439
00:24:47,120 --> 00:24:50,280
abdominal pain, nausea, and in rare cases,

440
00:24:50,380 --> 00:24:54,520
respiratory arrest, even death.

441
00:24:54,620 --> 00:24:56,020
When the shaman ate the mushroom,

442
00:24:56,120 --> 00:24:59,540
his kidneys filtered the muscarin from his urine.

443
00:24:59,640 --> 00:25:03,240
Their disciples would then drink the urine,

444
00:25:03,340 --> 00:25:06,140
which would be free of these negative compounds.

445
00:25:06,240 --> 00:25:08,540
And then they would have an experience that

446
00:25:08,640 --> 00:25:11,480
was a lot more pleasant.

447
00:25:11,481 --> 00:25:13,880
Reindeer also love these mushrooms.

448
00:25:13,980 --> 00:25:16,720
According to some legends, they may have caused Santa's reindeer

449
00:25:16,820 --> 00:25:22,220
to hallucinate and prance around as if they were flying.

450
00:25:22,320 --> 00:25:24,220
And don't forget the round guy in the suit

451
00:25:24,320 --> 00:25:26,920
with the white tufts.

452
00:25:27,020 --> 00:25:27,720
Look familiar?

453
00:25:31,740 --> 00:25:34,540
Perhaps not as colorful in appearance,

454
00:25:34,640 --> 00:25:37,340
this little mushroom is one of several fungi containing

455
00:25:37,440 --> 00:25:41,480
another mind-altering substance, psilocybin.

456
00:25:41,481 --> 00:25:45,080
Effects of psilocybin or psilocybin containing mushrooms

457
00:25:45,180 --> 00:25:48,820
on human beings is dependent upon dosage.

458
00:25:48,920 --> 00:25:53,600
But psilocybin does induce an altered state of consciousness,

459
00:25:53,700 --> 00:25:57,000
a visionary state.

460
00:25:57,100 --> 00:25:59,500
For this to occur in the brain,

461
00:25:59,600 --> 00:26:02,200
psilocybin metabolizes into psilocin,

462
00:26:02,300 --> 00:26:05,140
which has a similar structure as serotonin,

463
00:26:05,240 --> 00:26:09,400
a natural neurotransmitter affecting our mood.

464
00:26:09,401 --> 00:26:13,700
The silicin is then able to bind to serotonin's receptors,

465
00:26:13,800 --> 00:26:17,280
exciting nerves as if a massive rush of real serotonin

466
00:26:17,380 --> 00:26:19,140
had been released.

467
00:26:19,240 --> 00:26:21,340
The floodgates of the senses are open.

468
00:26:21,440 --> 00:26:24,640
Enormous amounts of information comes in.

469
00:26:24,740 --> 00:26:27,220
Mankind's use of hallucinogenic mushrooms,

470
00:26:27,320 --> 00:26:30,120
more commonly known today as magic mushrooms,

471
00:26:30,220 --> 00:26:34,220
can be traced back over 7,000 years through ancient artwork.

472
00:26:39,400 --> 00:26:42,600
To the Aztec civilization, magic mushrooms were center stage

473
00:26:42,700 --> 00:26:46,900
at many religious or healing ceremonies.

474
00:26:47,000 --> 00:26:49,580
That all changed in the 16th century,

475
00:26:49,680 --> 00:26:51,620
with the arrival of the Spanish conquistadors

476
00:26:51,720 --> 00:26:53,720
to the New World.

477
00:26:53,820 --> 00:26:57,360
The Spaniards condemned the use of plant hallucinogens

478
00:26:57,460 --> 00:27:00,280
to the harshest punishments of the Inquisition,

479
00:27:00,380 --> 00:27:02,360
including execution.

480
00:27:02,460 --> 00:27:04,820
Consequently, the use of these compounds

481
00:27:04,920 --> 00:27:06,900
by native peoples in the Americas

482
00:27:06,901 --> 00:27:10,800
went deeply underground for centuries.

483
00:27:10,900 --> 00:27:14,540
In May 1957, Life magazine ran an article

484
00:27:14,640 --> 00:27:17,340
discussing the contemporary use of psilocybin mushrooms

485
00:27:17,440 --> 00:27:18,940
in Mexico.

486
00:27:19,040 --> 00:27:21,440
Some psychology professors took note.

487
00:27:21,540 --> 00:27:24,180
They were all sort of epicentered at Harvard

488
00:27:24,280 --> 00:27:26,780
at the time when the use of these mushrooms

489
00:27:26,880 --> 00:27:31,360
was sort of a leaping out of the fabric of knowledge

490
00:27:31,460 --> 00:27:32,660
coming from Mexico.

491
00:27:32,760 --> 00:27:36,900
There was increasing interest within

492
00:27:36,901 --> 00:27:39,740
psychiatry, particularly among investigators,

493
00:27:39,840 --> 00:27:42,640
to examine the range of effects of a variety

494
00:27:42,740 --> 00:27:46,940
of psychedelic compounds, including psilocybin.

495
00:27:47,040 --> 00:27:49,540
By the 1960s, the use of psilocybin

496
00:27:49,640 --> 00:27:52,940
mushrooms and a compound derived from an ergot fungus,

497
00:27:53,040 --> 00:27:56,360
known as LSD, escaped the laboratory

498
00:27:56,460 --> 00:27:58,460
and leapt into the emerging counterculture.

499
00:28:01,200 --> 00:28:04,260
In 1968, the United States government reacted

500
00:28:04,261 --> 00:28:08,460
by making the possession of psilocybin mushrooms illegal.

501
00:28:08,560 --> 00:28:11,500
However, in the last 15 years or so,

502
00:28:11,600 --> 00:28:16,900
it's been possible to get approval to conduct careful,

503
00:28:17,000 --> 00:28:22,320
safe, tightly controlled, structured studies.

504
00:28:22,420 --> 00:28:25,220
This includes no longer taking psilocybin directly

505
00:28:25,320 --> 00:28:27,620
from mushrooms, but synthesizing it

506
00:28:27,720 --> 00:28:29,720
in federally approved laboratories that

507
00:28:29,820 --> 00:28:34,260
can create a more consistent dosage and potency.

508
00:28:34,261 --> 00:28:37,260
The studies include one by Dr. Charles Grobe

509
00:28:37,360 --> 00:28:40,100
at LA Biomedical Research Institute.

510
00:28:40,200 --> 00:28:42,100
CHARLES GROBE, LA Biomedical Research Institute.

511
00:28:42,200 --> 00:28:44,000
We've been allowed to treat people with advanced stage

512
00:28:44,100 --> 00:28:47,800
cancer who have overwhelming existential anxiety.

513
00:28:47,900 --> 00:28:51,340
And we're treating the anxiety, not the cancer per se.

514
00:28:51,440 --> 00:28:55,520
Our observations at this point are quite encouraging.

515
00:28:55,620 --> 00:28:58,980
Mushrooms may have gone to a few people's heads.

516
00:28:59,080 --> 00:29:02,360
But how did this little mold and its pal, the cantaloupe,

517
00:29:02,361 --> 00:29:05,360
pair up to save millions of lives.

518
00:29:10,360 --> 00:29:12,860
We like to eat some fungi.

519
00:29:12,861 --> 00:29:17,860
But then again, some fungi like to eat us.

520
00:29:17,861 --> 00:29:20,360
Although mold and fungus spores surround us,

521
00:29:20,361 --> 00:29:23,360
for the most part, they're harmless.

522
00:29:23,361 --> 00:29:27,860
But given the right conditions, some fungi can grow on us

523
00:29:27,861 --> 00:29:31,360
and within us, sometimes with devastating results.

524
00:29:31,361 --> 00:29:34,260
results.

525
00:29:34,360 --> 00:29:37,900
The fungus tinea pedis, or athlete's foot,

526
00:29:38,000 --> 00:29:41,300
is one of our most common fungal foes.

527
00:29:41,400 --> 00:29:45,400
It affects 75% of all Americans to some degree.

528
00:29:45,500 --> 00:29:48,440
With athlete's foot, they tend to be present on wet surfaces.

529
00:29:48,540 --> 00:29:52,940
The fungi then stick to the skin and then start growing,

530
00:29:53,040 --> 00:29:56,680
and that's why you get athlete's foot.

531
00:29:56,780 --> 00:29:59,480
For the fungus to survive and thrive on your foot,

532
00:29:59,481 --> 00:30:02,780
It needs constant moisture, often in the form of the sweat

533
00:30:02,880 --> 00:30:05,320
between your toes.

534
00:30:05,420 --> 00:30:08,260
There, it primarily consumes dead skin cells

535
00:30:08,360 --> 00:30:11,820
and spreads on the epidermal, or top layer of skin,

536
00:30:11,920 --> 00:30:15,960
potentially affecting the entire foot.

537
00:30:16,060 --> 00:30:17,960
While topical antifungal medicines

538
00:30:18,060 --> 00:30:20,500
can normally treat athlete's foot by either killing

539
00:30:20,600 --> 00:30:24,640
or inhibiting the fungal growth, much deeper fungal infections

540
00:30:24,740 --> 00:30:28,380
require more serious treatment.

541
00:30:28,381 --> 00:30:31,440
Doctors at the Los Angeles Biomedical Research Institute

542
00:30:31,520 --> 00:30:33,860
are interested in fighting a group of fungi

543
00:30:33,920 --> 00:30:35,460
that cause what is commonly referred

544
00:30:35,520 --> 00:30:39,120
to as opportunistic fungal infections.

545
00:30:39,200 --> 00:30:41,360
The main fungus that lives in the human body

546
00:30:41,420 --> 00:30:44,360
is members of the genus Candida, and it's

547
00:30:44,420 --> 00:30:48,200
part of the normal human flora.

548
00:30:48,260 --> 00:30:51,200
And it's typically found on the skin, in the mouth,

549
00:30:51,260 --> 00:30:53,700
and within our intestinal tract.

550
00:30:53,780 --> 00:30:56,100
So if something happens to perturb either system

551
00:30:56,101 --> 00:30:58,500
or something kills the bacterial flora,

552
00:30:58,600 --> 00:31:02,000
such as taking antibiotics, then the fungus can overgrow.

553
00:31:02,100 --> 00:31:04,600
If there's a weakened immune system,

554
00:31:04,700 --> 00:31:08,440
then it can actually cause a more serious infection.

555
00:31:08,540 --> 00:31:13,460
Opportunistic fungal infections emerged just over 50 years ago.

556
00:31:13,560 --> 00:31:15,960
They've grown along with advances in antibiotics

557
00:31:16,060 --> 00:31:19,560
and other medical treatments, such as abdominal surgery

558
00:31:19,660 --> 00:31:24,200
or chemotherapy that alter the body's natural chemistry

559
00:31:24,201 --> 00:31:26,960
and make way for certain fungi to spread through the body.

560
00:31:31,140 --> 00:31:34,680
Candida actually adheres to and invades the tissue surrounding

561
00:31:34,681 --> 00:31:36,580
it.

562
00:31:36,581 --> 00:31:38,420
The fungus then uses the bloodstream

563
00:31:38,421 --> 00:31:42,180
to travel throughout the body, commonly infecting areas such

564
00:31:42,181 --> 00:31:49,260
as the lungs, liver, spleen, brain, eyes, and even bone.

565
00:31:49,261 --> 00:31:53,060
When untreated, these infections can cause severe pain, fever,

566
00:31:53,061 --> 00:31:57,300
loss of vision, pneumonia, even death.

567
00:31:57,400 --> 00:31:59,100
The biggest challenge for treatment

568
00:31:59,200 --> 00:32:02,600
of serious fungal infections is that their cell structure

569
00:32:02,700 --> 00:32:05,400
is very similar to the human cell.

570
00:32:05,500 --> 00:32:07,800
So the name of the game with the fungal infections

571
00:32:07,900 --> 00:32:11,520
has been to find agents that will kill these complex cells

572
00:32:11,620 --> 00:32:15,520
but not cause damage to the human cells.

573
00:32:15,620 --> 00:32:18,960
Ironically, the rise in opportunistic fungal infections

574
00:32:19,060 --> 00:32:21,420
is due in part to the influence of the most famous

575
00:32:21,421 --> 00:32:25,120
and beneficial fungus in history, penicillium.

576
00:32:28,120 --> 00:32:32,660
In 1939, nearly 12 years after its accidental discovery,

577
00:32:32,661 --> 00:32:36,460
a group of Oxford scientists, including Dr. Howard Florey,

578
00:32:36,461 --> 00:32:39,080
isolated a potent strain of the mold,

579
00:32:39,081 --> 00:32:42,380
often found on fruits and cheeses.

580
00:32:42,381 --> 00:32:46,920
It became the first antibiotic, penicillin.

581
00:32:46,921 --> 00:32:49,580
The next challenge, producing this new miracle cure

582
00:32:49,581 --> 00:32:52,880
on a larger scale. Europe was now headed toward

583
00:32:52,980 --> 00:32:55,980
the beginning of World War II. There was great concern if the

584
00:32:56,080 --> 00:32:59,020
work were done in England, would they be invaded?

585
00:32:59,120 --> 00:33:03,320
Would they be bombed? Where was a safe place?

586
00:33:03,420 --> 00:33:07,040
The British scientists decided on the United States.

587
00:33:07,140 --> 00:33:09,940
But before they set off on their trip, they rubbed

588
00:33:10,040 --> 00:33:13,600
penicillium mold into their coats in case their freeze-dried

589
00:33:13,700 --> 00:33:17,540
samples were confiscated by enemy agents.

590
00:33:17,541 --> 00:33:21,240
After arriving successfully at the USDA's Northern Regional

591
00:33:21,241 --> 00:33:23,940
Research Center in Peoria, Illinois,

592
00:33:23,941 --> 00:33:26,880
they encountered another problem.

593
00:33:26,881 --> 00:33:31,380
Their strain, while powerful, wasn't powerful enough.

594
00:33:31,381 --> 00:33:34,900
So various people in Peoria went to the fruit markets

595
00:33:34,901 --> 00:33:37,400
looking for old moldy fruit.

596
00:33:37,401 --> 00:33:41,000
And on one of those pieces of fruit, a cantaloupe,

597
00:33:41,001 --> 00:33:44,440
was a green mold, a green mold that looked just like the

598
00:33:44,441 --> 00:33:48,740
like the penicillium that was producing penicillin.

599
00:33:48,840 --> 00:33:52,140
And so they brought it back to this lab, isolated it,

600
00:33:52,240 --> 00:33:58,380
and that turned out to be a very high producer of penicillin.

601
00:33:58,480 --> 00:34:00,920
So with this stronger penicillium strain,

602
00:34:01,020 --> 00:34:03,420
the researchers ramped up production.

603
00:34:03,520 --> 00:34:07,060
The rest, as they say, is history.

604
00:34:07,160 --> 00:34:09,400
We are talking about millions and millions of people

605
00:34:09,500 --> 00:34:11,040
being saved by this drug.

606
00:34:11,041 --> 00:34:15,380
And in addition, it paved the way for looking for other

607
00:34:15,480 --> 00:34:18,780
pharmaceuticals from fungi.

608
00:34:18,880 --> 00:34:21,320
That's the mission of Montana State University plant

609
00:34:21,420 --> 00:34:25,280
scientist Gary Strobel, who looks for beneficial fungi

610
00:34:25,380 --> 00:34:28,720
where few people do, deep within plants found

611
00:34:28,820 --> 00:34:31,720
in the world's forests.

612
00:34:31,820 --> 00:34:34,400
Over the last 20 years, Strobel has traveled

613
00:34:34,500 --> 00:34:37,060
to over 80 countries where he and his team

614
00:34:37,160 --> 00:34:39,600
have discovered over 1,000 fungi.

615
00:34:39,601 --> 00:34:43,000
There's life all over this planet, much of which

616
00:34:43,100 --> 00:34:45,900
has gone undiscovered, and much of which

617
00:34:46,000 --> 00:34:48,500
is in these areas of high diversity.

618
00:34:48,600 --> 00:34:54,340
These things are gorgeous, all colors and shapes and sizes.

619
00:34:54,440 --> 00:34:56,760
A single tree is likely to contain thousands

620
00:34:56,860 --> 00:34:58,360
of different fungi.

621
00:34:58,460 --> 00:35:00,260
What we're, of course, trying to do

622
00:35:00,360 --> 00:35:05,300
is to particularly isolate these fungi, grow the fungi,

623
00:35:05,400 --> 00:35:08,400
and see what products they make.

624
00:35:08,401 --> 00:35:10,740
How successful has Strobel been?

625
00:35:10,840 --> 00:35:11,840
Very.

626
00:35:11,940 --> 00:35:13,340
He's made breakthrough discoveries

627
00:35:13,440 --> 00:35:16,400
in the fields of medicine, environmental cleanup,

628
00:35:16,500 --> 00:35:18,640
and energy production.

629
00:35:18,740 --> 00:35:22,040
As a nod to his work, the Smithsonian's American History

630
00:35:22,140 --> 00:35:24,780
Collection now contains the first of his distinctive red

631
00:35:24,880 --> 00:35:29,360
caps, often used by Strobel to collect samples in the field.

632
00:35:29,460 --> 00:35:31,720
The next step, of course, is to put all of these

633
00:35:31,820 --> 00:35:37,460
in plastic bags, load them up, take them back to the laboratory

634
00:35:37,461 --> 00:35:39,460
where they're processed.

635
00:35:39,461 --> 00:35:41,460
We're interested only in the microorganisms,

636
00:35:41,461 --> 00:35:43,460
the fungi that are living inside

637
00:35:43,461 --> 00:35:46,460
and associated with the tissues of the plant.

638
00:35:46,461 --> 00:35:50,460
We'll have to surface sterilize the outside.

639
00:35:50,461 --> 00:35:54,460
And the next step is to dig into the tissues on the stem itself

640
00:35:54,461 --> 00:35:57,460
and place those on auger plates.

641
00:35:57,461 --> 00:36:00,460
And after a few days, the hyphae,

642
00:36:00,461 --> 00:36:05,460
or the threads of the fungus, move out onto the auger plate.

643
00:36:05,461 --> 00:36:09,900
So this is simply an example of going to the forest,

644
00:36:09,901 --> 00:36:13,100
finding something peculiar, and then following up

645
00:36:13,101 --> 00:36:16,240
with an extreme amount of lab work.

646
00:36:16,241 --> 00:36:20,680
We just have just untold numbers of these organisms.

647
00:36:20,681 --> 00:36:24,920
And so it's a huge opportunity for somebody going into science

648
00:36:24,921 --> 00:36:28,020
to make a case for studying fungi.

649
00:36:28,021 --> 00:36:32,120
And studying fungi may not only save lives,

650
00:36:32,121 --> 00:36:34,660
but could also solve a global fuel crisis.

651
00:36:40,460 --> 00:36:45,400
At the Fungi Perfecti facility outside of Olympia, Washington,

652
00:36:45,401 --> 00:36:49,000
fungi guru Paul Stamets is in the hunt for new ways

653
00:36:49,001 --> 00:36:53,100
to utilize the amazing mushroom.

654
00:36:53,101 --> 00:36:55,720
These are burlap sacks full of wood chips,

655
00:36:55,721 --> 00:36:58,680
but they're covered with oyster mushroom mycelium.

656
00:36:58,681 --> 00:37:01,820
And these are used for filtration of water.

657
00:37:01,821 --> 00:37:07,100
So when surface water runs into storm drains or into rivers,

658
00:37:07,101 --> 00:37:10,600
then the silt and the coliform bacteria,

659
00:37:10,601 --> 00:37:12,920
which is a huge problem for the shellfish industry

660
00:37:12,921 --> 00:37:17,240
and salmon recovery, is captured by the burlap sacks.

661
00:37:17,241 --> 00:37:21,280
And then the oyster mushroom mycelium gobbles it up.

662
00:37:21,281 --> 00:37:23,060
The mycelium will eat the bacteria

663
00:37:23,061 --> 00:37:27,860
and break down petroleum products, diesel, oil,

664
00:37:27,861 --> 00:37:29,400
even a lot of pesticides.

665
00:37:29,401 --> 00:37:31,700
It actually uses it as a food source.

666
00:37:31,701 --> 00:37:34,700
benefits the ecosystem because it feeds the food chains of many

667
00:37:34,800 --> 00:37:37,940
other organisms.

668
00:37:38,040 --> 00:37:40,700
Currently, Stamets mycofiltration mycelium

669
00:37:40,800 --> 00:37:44,440
bags are employed at roughly six pilot projects in Washington

670
00:37:44,540 --> 00:37:47,940
State and British Columbia, with impressive results in reducing

671
00:37:48,040 --> 00:37:53,860
coliform bacteria, including E. coli.

672
00:37:53,960 --> 00:37:58,960
This is about 2,000 pounds of mycelium, oyster mushroom

673
00:37:58,961 --> 00:38:02,900
mushroom mycelium, we create sort of mycelial tsunamis

674
00:38:03,000 --> 00:38:05,460
that we generate out of this facility,

675
00:38:05,560 --> 00:38:10,140
up to 10,000 kilos of mycelium per week.

676
00:38:10,240 --> 00:38:12,340
This mycelium is sent all over the country

677
00:38:12,440 --> 00:38:14,080
for various environmental projects.

678
00:38:16,740 --> 00:38:20,380
In Big Sky country, clean waste of Belgrade, Montana

679
00:38:20,480 --> 00:38:26,420
uses fungi to kill the E. coli bacteria found in human waste,

680
00:38:26,421 --> 00:38:29,160
aptly named poo powder.

681
00:38:29,260 --> 00:38:34,420
It's sold as part of a waste bag kit called a wag bag.

682
00:38:34,520 --> 00:38:36,960
Let me kind of show you how that works.

683
00:38:37,060 --> 00:38:41,540
Inside our waste bag kit, toilet in a bag,

684
00:38:41,640 --> 00:38:46,000
are an outer bag, an inner bag, a hand sanitizer,

685
00:38:46,100 --> 00:38:48,100
and some toilet paper.

686
00:38:48,200 --> 00:38:49,940
This is our funnel bag.

687
00:38:50,040 --> 00:38:52,720
Preloaded in the funnel bag is our poo powder.

688
00:38:52,721 --> 00:38:57,160
The key bacteria-killing ingredient in poo powder

689
00:38:57,260 --> 00:39:01,460
comes from the fungus Muscador albus.

690
00:39:01,560 --> 00:39:04,240
Gary Strobel spotted it in a cinnamon tree

691
00:39:04,340 --> 00:39:06,700
while hunting for fungi in Honduras.

692
00:39:06,800 --> 00:39:09,740
We figured out a way of making it survive and grow

693
00:39:09,840 --> 00:39:13,700
in the waste, and we add another organism, another fungus,

694
00:39:13,800 --> 00:39:17,280
from the rainforests of Peru that bring about the degradation

695
00:39:17,380 --> 00:39:18,680
of the waste.

696
00:39:18,780 --> 00:39:21,480
And these agents together, when they are

697
00:39:21,481 --> 00:39:25,620
when they are activated by warmth and moisture, urine mostly,

698
00:39:26,140 --> 00:39:30,480
release a gas which kills E. coli naturally

699
00:39:30,481 --> 00:39:32,360
in a way that's non-toxic to humans.

700
00:39:33,700 --> 00:39:36,080
A mixing machine combines these fungi

701
00:39:36,081 --> 00:39:37,800
with other earth-friendly ingredients

702
00:39:37,801 --> 00:39:41,460
that help gel, deodorize, and catalyze the waste.

703
00:39:43,520 --> 00:39:44,840
After several minutes,

704
00:39:45,320 --> 00:39:47,180
out comes poo powder for the waste bag.

705
00:39:48,240 --> 00:39:50,620
When the person is done using the toilet,

706
00:39:50,621 --> 00:39:57,120
the bag itself, just fold it up, placed inside the outer bag,

707
00:39:57,220 --> 00:39:58,560
zip-sealed closed.

708
00:39:58,660 --> 00:40:00,800
Both bags are made in a biodegradable way

709
00:40:00,900 --> 00:40:03,540
with starch derivatives so that they're degradable,

710
00:40:03,640 --> 00:40:05,240
safer than a baby diaper.

711
00:40:05,340 --> 00:40:06,940
What we would do with this is what we've just done.

712
00:40:07,040 --> 00:40:11,500
Use it, seal it, and toss it.

713
00:40:11,600 --> 00:40:14,980
On the day of our visit, over 13 million wag bag

714
00:40:15,080 --> 00:40:18,020
kits were shipped to a large military supply depot

715
00:40:18,021 --> 00:40:21,020
in Philadelphia, Pennsylvania.

716
00:40:21,120 --> 00:40:23,520
Half of our business is the US military and FEMA

717
00:40:23,620 --> 00:40:25,820
for emergency and disaster relief.

718
00:40:25,920 --> 00:40:28,960
We also have a strong presence in the outdoor retail market,

719
00:40:29,060 --> 00:40:32,260
camping, and a very rapidly growing market in health care,

720
00:40:32,360 --> 00:40:37,480
hospitals, nursing homes, and home health care.

721
00:40:37,580 --> 00:40:40,580
Scientists at the Department of Energy's Pacific Northwest

722
00:40:40,680 --> 00:40:43,480
National Laboratory in Richland, Washington,

723
00:40:43,580 --> 00:40:47,680
are working on another front in fungal studies.

724
00:40:47,681 --> 00:40:50,420
They hope that fungi's insatiable appetite

725
00:40:50,520 --> 00:40:53,720
will be able to convert cellulose to ethanol.

726
00:40:53,820 --> 00:40:57,160
That's the holy grail of biofuel.

727
00:40:57,260 --> 00:40:58,760
We're looking at cutting our dependence

728
00:40:58,860 --> 00:41:01,560
on foreign petroleum and petroleum in general

729
00:41:01,660 --> 00:41:04,660
by using organisms such as Aspergillus niger

730
00:41:04,760 --> 00:41:07,660
and Aspergillus arisum and Trichoderma reesei

731
00:41:07,760 --> 00:41:11,400
to produce enzymes, fuels, and organic acids that can

732
00:41:11,500 --> 00:41:14,700
displace foreign fuels.

733
00:41:14,800 --> 00:41:16,200
The lab's interest in the fungus

734
00:41:16,201 --> 00:41:19,900
trichoderma reesei stems from the fungi's history of devouring

735
00:41:20,000 --> 00:41:22,400
complex materials.

736
00:41:22,500 --> 00:41:25,000
Trichoderma reesei is a fungus that was discovered in the

737
00:41:25,100 --> 00:41:28,840
40s breaking down tent canvases in the Pacific theater

738
00:41:28,940 --> 00:41:30,640
during World War II.

739
00:41:30,740 --> 00:41:33,520
Well, the reason it could do that is it was digesting

740
00:41:33,620 --> 00:41:35,180
the cellulose.

741
00:41:35,280 --> 00:41:39,360
And over the years, people have developed strains of trichoderma

742
00:41:39,460 --> 00:41:43,400
reesei that produce those cellulases, those enzymes,

743
00:41:43,401 --> 00:41:47,400
that speed up the reactions of breaking down cellulose

744
00:41:47,500 --> 00:41:50,140
into simple sugars.

745
00:41:50,240 --> 00:41:51,800
And once you've got sugar, it's

746
00:41:51,900 --> 00:41:55,740
easy to convert it to ethanol.

747
00:41:55,840 --> 00:41:57,240
The next challenge for the lab will

748
00:41:57,340 --> 00:41:59,980
be to find a way to produce the enzymes and fuel

749
00:42:00,080 --> 00:42:01,880
efficiently on a large scale.

750
00:42:06,860 --> 00:42:10,220
Gary Strobel looks for fuel in another fungus.

751
00:42:10,320 --> 00:42:13,160
This fungus called C13 and the product that it's

752
00:42:13,161 --> 00:42:16,160
is mycodiesel, a new term.

753
00:42:16,260 --> 00:42:17,160
It's not biodiesel.

754
00:42:17,260 --> 00:42:22,900
Biodiesel are oils and fatty substances from higher plants.

755
00:42:23,000 --> 00:42:25,600
This is actually diesel.

756
00:42:25,700 --> 00:42:29,920
This fungus is making many of the ingredients in diesel fuel,

757
00:42:30,020 --> 00:42:33,040
and it's being made in a quantity that possibly

758
00:42:33,140 --> 00:42:34,820
could be commercially feasible.

759
00:42:34,920 --> 00:42:37,960
When I saw the data, I think every hair on my body,

760
00:42:38,060 --> 00:42:41,220
including these, stood straight up.

761
00:42:41,221 --> 00:42:44,120
The day when you fill up your car with fungi fuel

762
00:42:44,220 --> 00:42:46,660
may not be that far off into the future.

763
00:42:46,760 --> 00:42:49,960
So maybe we'll start a company called Strobe Oil.

764
00:42:52,460 --> 00:42:55,460
Whether you view them as friend or foe,

765
00:42:55,560 --> 00:42:58,580
fungi have been on Earth for billions of years

766
00:42:58,680 --> 00:43:02,080
and will likely be here long after we're gone.

767
00:43:02,180 --> 00:43:06,420
So perhaps it's time to embrace our fungal future.

768
00:43:06,520 --> 00:43:09,720
After all, they're waiting for us.

769
00:43:09,721 --> 00:43:13,960
Ultimately, each of us will become part fungus.

770
00:43:14,060 --> 00:43:16,920
When we die, fungi are going to infuse us.

